<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
   Bounds the disk use of ClickHouse's own system log tables with a TTL, and changes nothing else.

   Mounted read-only into /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/ by clickhouse.compose.yaml. ClickHouse
   merges every file in config.d over config.xml (documented at
   https://clickhouse.com/docs/operations/configuration-files), so each element below adds one
   child to the image's own element of the same name and leaves its other children (flush interval,
   buffer sizes, partition key, level) as the image has them.

   The TTL setting used: the "ttl" element of a system log table. The system tables overview
   (https://clickhouse.com/docs/operations/system-tables/overview) lists it among the elements a
   config file may set ("ttl: specify table TTL expression") and says "By default, table growth is
   unlimited. To control a size of a table, you can use TTL settings for removing outdated log
   records." The expression is a MergeTree table TTL
   (https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/statements/alter/ttl); expired rows are removed when
   ClickHouse merges data parts, and it starts an off-schedule merge when it sees data has
   expired (https://clickhouse.com/docs/engines/table-engines/mergetree-family/mergetree, section
   "Removing expired data"). Those pages are the live docs site, which is newer than 26.3 (it lists
   settings added in 26.10); the same element, expression form and merge behaviour were checked on
   the 26.3.39.7 server itself (system.tables.engine_full shows the TTL clause on every table).

   Why this file exists: the v6 benchmark runs switched 21 of these tables off, which made
   ClickHouse 16 percent faster at 8 searchers than at its defaults (423 to 493 QPS, v5 against v6)
   and no report said so. The 26.3.39.7 image writes query_log, text_log, trace_log and
   processors_profile_log rows for every search, and a search carries its 1024-float vector inline
   in the SQL, so the logs run to about 30 KB per search (measured 2026-10-05: 12,300 searches left
   364 MiB of active log parts, text_log 218 MiB and query_log 139 MiB of it). That is the engine's
   real per-search cost at its defaults, and the benchmark has to pay it. What it must not do is fill the disk: a few
   benchmark runs left 3.4 GiB of active parts (6.1 GB on disk) on 2026-10-05.

   What it does: every log table the image config switches on gets "event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR
   DELETE" (the span log has no event_time column and gets finish_date, see below). The 1 hour is
   the same text the engine description prints (ClickHouseSink.LOG_TTL_TEXT); a test fails if they
   differ. Every table is still on, flushed at its image-default interval, with its image-default
   partitioning. The image already gives five of these tables a TTL of 3 or 30 days; those are
   replaced by the 1 hour, so no table is left with a longer bound. Nothing is removed and nothing
   else is changed, including the server's own log file, which keeps the image's rotation.

   What it does not do: it does not keep a run's disk use under any fixed size. A benchmark search
   writes more than a TTL can expire while the run lasts (three cycles of 4,000 searches wrote about
   550 to 675 MB in about two minutes), and ClickHouse keeps expired parts on disk for
   old_parts_lifetime, 480 s, before it deletes the files. The bound is time: log rows older than the
   TTL are dropped by the next merge, and their files go about 8 minutes later. Rows are only dropped
   while the server runs, so after a run the logs stay on disk until the next start.

   The span log is the one table the image defines with an engine text, and ClickHouse refuses a
   ttl element next to an engine element, so its TTL is added to the engine text itself, which
   repeats the image's definition word for word. That table is empty unless tracing is on.
   instrumentation_trace_log is in the image config but the 26.3.39.7 server does not create it.

   When the TTL changes, ClickHouse renames the existing table to name_0 at the first flush and
   starts a new one, and the renamed copy never expires. After the first start with a changed TTL
   run clickhouse-config/clear-system-logs.sh once. A change to this file needs
   docker compose up -d with the force-recreate flag.
-->
<clickhouse>
   <query_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </query_log>
   <trace_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </trace_log>
   <query_thread_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </query_thread_log>
   <query_views_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </query_views_log>
   <part_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </part_log>
   <background_schedule_pool_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </background_schedule_pool_log>
   <text_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </text_log>
   <metric_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </metric_log>
   <error_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </error_log>
   <instrumentation_trace_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </instrumentation_trace_log>
   <query_metric_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </query_metric_log>
   <asynchronous_metric_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </asynchronous_metric_log>
   <iceberg_metadata_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </iceberg_metadata_log>
   <delta_lake_metadata_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </delta_lake_metadata_log>
   <opentelemetry_span_log>
      <engine>engine MergeTree partition by toYYYYMM(finish_date) order by (finish_date, finish_time_us) ttl finish_date + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</engine>
   </opentelemetry_span_log>
   <crash_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </crash_log>
   <processors_profile_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </processors_profile_log>
   <asynchronous_insert_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </asynchronous_insert_log>
   <backup_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </backup_log>
   <s3queue_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </s3queue_log>
   <blob_storage_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </blob_storage_log>
   <aggregated_zookeeper_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </aggregated_zookeeper_log>
   <zookeeper_connection_log>
      <ttl>event_time + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
   </zookeeper_connection_log>
</clickhouse>
